 ],
 "literal": "Genre: Vākai, Sub-theme: Arasa Vākai / As the terrifying demons took in a group and hid / the sun of far-shining excellence, not seeing it, / in the circular world which lost its eye of light to darkness, / to end the distress and affliction, he of fierce power, / the black-hued one, brought it back and restored it, just like that / at the time of distress when he was without his kingdom, / with the courtyard where drums sound and roar, dashing against the banks / the roaring water of the surging, abundantly great Kaveri / to end the distress of the fertile good country, / sung by Kapilar of the untruthless tongue, / there on the tall mountain adorned with clouds, swiftly / seeing the retreating backs of the warriors who desired battle, / high upon Mullur of blameless excellence, / the greatly victorious Valavan who was on the difficult path, / having shown his moon-resembling white umbrella, that umbrella / O you who excel in fame, establishing it with novelty! / On the peak where the cave tiger is engraved, wearing glowing ornaments / and a garland buzzing with bees, your greatly famed elder brother, / to enjoy the good deeds done here, having gone there, / because he departed to the world of the noble ones, / on the side of the paths, searching the directions / with an anxious heart, to end our sorrow, / you appeared indeed, O lord with arranged garlands! / As the rocky surface crumbled and the forest burned, / as many ponds in the domain of abundant water dried up, / at the time of prolonged summer lacking greenness, / the vast earth softening, having gathered, / just like the rain having clouds of roaring thunder pours!",
 "translation": "Just as the dark-hued Lord of fierce power brought back and restored the sun when the terrifying demons had gathered and hidden its far-shining glory, leaving the circular world in blind darkness and deep distress, so too, when the Chola king Valavan, having lost his kingdom, was in exile on the difficult, high paths of Mullur hill—the hill of blameless excellence sung by Kapilar of the untruthless tongue, where the tall peaks are adorned with clouds and where warriors desiring battle swiftly turned their backs in retreat—you showed your moon-like white umbrella and established it anew, O you who excel in fame! When your greatly famed elder brother, who wore glowing ornaments and a bee-buzzing garland, and had the cave-tiger emblem engraved on his peaks, departed to the world of the gods to enjoy the fruits of his good deeds done here, we were left wandering the paths, searching all directions with anxious hearts. To end our deep sorrow, you appeared, O lord adorned with arranged garlands! Your arrival is like the sudden pouring of thunder-roaring rain-clouds upon the vast, parched earth, softening it, after a prolonged summer has stripped away all greenness, crumbled the rocks, scorched the forests, and dried up the many ponds of this water-rich land.",
 "modern": "அச்சம் தரும் அசுரர்கள் கூட்டமாகச் சேர்ந்து, வானில் விளங்கும் ஞாயிற்றை மறைத்ததால், உலகம் இருளில் மூழ்கித் தவித்தது. அப்போது, கரிய நிறமுடைய திருமால் அந்த ஞாயிற்றை மீண்டும் கொண்டு வந்து உலகத்தின் துயரத்தைத் தீர்த்தார். அதுபோல, சோழ மன்னன் தன் அரசை இழந்து அல்லல் உற்ற காலத்தில், பொய்யா நாவுடைய கபிலர் பாடிய மேகங்கள் சூழ்ந்த முள்ளூர் மலையின் உச்சியில், போரை விரும்பிய வீரர்கள் புறமுதுகிட்டு ஓடுமாறு செய்து, தன் வெண்கொற்றக் குடையைக் காட்டி, புதிய முறையில் ஆட்சியை நிலைநிறுத்திய புகழாளனே! குகைப்புலியைத் தன் கொடியில் பொறித்தவனும், ஒளிவீசும் அணிகலன்களையும் வண்டுகள் மொய்க்கும் மாலையையும் அணிந்தவனுமான உனது புகழ்பெற்ற அண்ணன், தான் செய்த நல்வினைகளின் பயனை நுகரத் தேவருலகம் சென்றான். அதனால், வழிகளில் திசை தெரியாமல் அலைந்து, கவலை தோய்ந்த நெஞ்சத்தோடு வருந்திய எங்களது துன்பம் தீர நீ தோன்றினாய். பாறைகள் பொடியாகி, காடுகள் வெம்பி, நீர்நிலைகள் வறண்டு, பசுமை அழிந்த நீண்ட கோடைக் காலத்தில், பூமி குளிருமாறு இடி முழக்கத்துடன் பொழியும் மழையைப் போல நீ வந்து எங்களைக் காத்தாய்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Māṟōkkattu Nappacalaiyār addresses Malaiyamāṉ Cōḻiya Vēṉāti Tirukkaṇṇaṉ in a time of deep grief. Tirukkaṇṇaṉ's illustrious elder brother (the chieftain Kāri) has passed away, leaving the land and its poets in despair, wandering like lost travelers. The poet compares this dark period to the mythical time when demons stole the sun, and praises Tirukkaṇṇaṉ's rise to leadership as the return of the sun by Vishnu. Tirukkaṇṇaṉ had previously shown his valor by helping the exiled Chola king (Valavan) regain his throne under a new white umbrella. His timely arrival to lead his people now is beautifully compared to life-giving rain pouring over a parched, summer-scorched landscape, bringing relief and softening the earth. This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai, celebrating the righteous victory and noble lineage of a leader.",
